1
00:00:12,657 --> 00:00:16,459
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a continuation of Majjhima Nikaya No.

2
00:00:16,559 --> 00:00:16,659
[SPEAKER_01]: 2.

3
00:00:19,501 --> 00:00:22,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

4
00:00:22,422 --> 00:00:26,684
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll just recap and continue sort of where we left off.

5
00:00:29,685 --> 00:00:30,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, the Buddha's just...

6
00:00:30,886 --> 00:00:39,510
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a teaching, where he's teaching us how to restrain the asavas and how to abandon the asavas.

7
00:00:41,860 --> 00:01:06,357
[SPEAKER_01]: And he began by saying that the destruction of these asavas, these defilements, is for one who knows and sees... for one who knows and sees attention with perspective or attention without... and what is attention without perspective.

8
00:01:06,517 --> 00:01:07,297
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he knows the difference.

9
00:01:10,308 --> 00:01:16,955
[SPEAKER_01]: And then we went to the first way to abandon these defilements is by seeing.

10
00:01:19,298 --> 00:01:20,639
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Pali word is dasana.

11
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:26,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So, defilements to be abandoned by dasana.

12
00:01:28,868 --> 00:01:31,811
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I looked it up and it's like...

13
00:01:34,017 --> 00:01:38,361
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems to suggest that the word apperception is a good translation for that.

14
00:01:39,122 --> 00:01:40,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing, apperception.

15
00:01:41,284 --> 00:01:49,411
[SPEAKER_01]: They're like observing things and then, over time, seeing where they rooted your actions and so on.

16
00:01:50,593 --> 00:01:58,700
[SPEAKER_01]: And you were saying the other day that this practice is basically about changing the significance of a thing.

17
00:02:00,373 --> 00:02:01,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, yeah.

18
00:02:01,954 --> 00:02:11,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, like, our perception is that, like, through the experience of certain perceptions, you get to understand more.

19
00:02:11,443 --> 00:02:17,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, next time you perceive the same thing, you don't have to go through the same motions of recollecting what you understood.

20
00:02:17,849 --> 00:02:23,393
[SPEAKER_00]: Understanding, knowledge, becomes part of, now, that perception, becomes a significance of what you're seeing.

21
00:02:24,134 --> 00:02:26,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not perceiving your knowledge,

22
00:02:27,403 --> 00:02:34,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Directly, like it's not written in this thing, but because of the effort you made, the knowledge now becomes a significance of your perception.

23
00:02:35,048 --> 00:02:35,869
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha said.

24
00:02:37,130 --> 00:02:39,832
[SPEAKER_00]: The knowledge is the result of perception.

25
00:02:40,032 --> 00:02:41,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception comes first.

26
00:02:41,113 --> 00:02:43,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Having perceived, one then gets to develop knowledge about it.

27
00:02:44,375 --> 00:02:54,862
[SPEAKER_00]: But once you know it, you don't need to keep telling yourself, oh, let me now get the knowledge of this which I'm perceiving, because perceiving it will mean that knowledge is present.

28
00:02:55,062 --> 00:02:59,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's... well, that's at least how I understand our perception, as a term.

29
00:02:59,505 --> 00:03:03,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So... and, yeah, that knowledge is on the level of changing the significance of things.

30
00:03:03,768 --> 00:03:05,369
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing a beautiful object,

31
00:03:06,290 --> 00:03:12,252
[SPEAKER_00]: For a mind that's not trained, results in, basically, significance of sensuality.

32
00:03:12,272 --> 00:03:21,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Significance of, oh, gratification of my pleasure, joy I can get if I have this beautiful object, fear of losing the beautiful object, you might have to protect it.

33
00:03:21,675 --> 00:03:23,916
[SPEAKER_00]: All that is significance of seeing the beautiful object.

34
00:03:24,496 --> 00:03:33,599
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you've been practicing sensory strain long enough, understanding dhamma sufficiently enough, developing mindfulness to the extent necessary, perception of beautiful will be signifying danger.

35
00:03:34,546 --> 00:03:41,470
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be signifying peril, like the Buddha said, like a... like a... what is it?

36
00:03:41,490 --> 00:03:43,511
[SPEAKER_00]: The pit filled with burning embers.

37
00:03:43,571 --> 00:03:44,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Charcoal pit.

38
00:03:44,231 --> 00:03:45,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, charcoal pit filled with burning embers.

39
00:03:46,653 --> 00:03:48,374
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the same centrality.

40
00:03:48,674 --> 00:03:50,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Now the man sees it, he sees for what it is.

41
00:03:50,955 --> 00:03:58,319
[SPEAKER_00]: And if somebody, if two strong men would come and pick you up and carry you, to throw you in a charcoal pit, like your intention would be everywhere else.

42
00:03:58,639 --> 00:04:00,500
[SPEAKER_00]: You would want to be anywhere else except there.

43
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:03,062
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't want to go into the pit of burning embers.

44
00:04:03,702 --> 00:04:09,104
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how the significance of sensuality must be developed in order for you to abandon sensuality.

45
00:04:09,244 --> 00:04:13,005
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to start seeing it as a charcoal pit full of burning embers.

46
00:04:13,465 --> 00:04:19,227
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mind will completely, completely not want to even entertain it for a split second, so to speak.

47
00:04:20,168 --> 00:04:28,873
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you cultivate that sufficiently enough, because sensuality is, factually, charcoal pit with burning embers, that will become apparent.

48
00:04:28,913 --> 00:04:33,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like the danger became apparent, the escape became apparent, and all these things that we will talk about.

49
00:04:33,956 --> 00:04:37,137
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the next time you see a sensual thing, that's that apperception.

50
00:04:37,237 --> 00:04:39,639
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not perceiving the beautiful anymore.

51
00:04:39,659 --> 00:04:45,202
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still the same thing you're perceiving, by the way, but now your perception with that knowledge is seeing the danger and the ugly.

52
00:04:46,807 --> 00:04:50,009
[SPEAKER_01]: You have changed the significance of that perception.

53
00:04:50,049 --> 00:04:53,871
[SPEAKER_01]: You've undone those significances of...

54
00:04:54,192 --> 00:04:54,512
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

55
00:04:54,812 --> 00:04:57,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Because those significances were not set in stone.

56
00:04:57,373 --> 00:04:59,915
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were, freedom from sensuality would not be possible.

57
00:05:00,375 --> 00:05:08,840
[SPEAKER_00]: They were simply maintained through your perpetual engagement, careless engagement and ignorant engagement with desire and sense gratification.

58
00:05:11,165 --> 00:05:18,688
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you you kind of start off, obviously, full of lust and ignorance.

59
00:05:18,788 --> 00:05:19,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

60
00:05:20,689 --> 00:05:23,631
[SPEAKER_01]: And seeing your body as a wonderful thing.

61
00:05:24,931 --> 00:05:26,812
[SPEAKER_00]: And as a tool of getting more wonderful things.

62
00:05:26,832 --> 00:05:28,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

63
00:05:28,113 --> 00:05:39,398
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you need to... the practice is to resignify it, or take apart that significance there, by looking at it for what it is.

64
00:05:40,315 --> 00:05:40,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

65
00:05:40,856 --> 00:05:45,640
[SPEAKER_00]: And in order for you to start seeing it for what it is, you need to start restraining your body first.

66
00:05:46,221 --> 00:05:48,763
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're still regarding sensuality as beautiful.

67
00:05:50,385 --> 00:05:52,407
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like... that's one layer, so to speak.

68
00:05:53,288 --> 00:06:01,416
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, acting out of body towards more gratification of sensuality, seeking more sensual object, that's another layer you add on top of the layer of your thought.

69
00:06:03,404 --> 00:06:14,488
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as long as any of those layers are there, you are fueling that misperception, that wrong significance, that mistaken notion of centrality as beautiful and non-harmful.

70
00:06:15,728 --> 00:06:20,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to first start restraining the coarser layer, which is layer of physical action.

71
00:06:21,770 --> 00:06:32,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you mentally still don't see a reason for it, and you're still attracted to it, and you still don't see it as dangerous or perilous, you need to restrain your body first, pretty much out of faith.

72
00:06:34,497 --> 00:06:37,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, until you get to understand it for yourself, and then you're the same.

73
00:06:37,739 --> 00:06:49,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, we said that many times before, you come with a horrible skin disease, it itches like hell, you go to see a doctor, the doctor gives you medicine, but says, well, before you start applying the medicine, you need to stop scratching it.

74
00:06:50,397 --> 00:06:58,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't matter the amount of medicine you apply, the amount of your meditation you do, and suttas you read, if you keep scratching your itch, you are aggravating your condition.

75
00:06:59,122 --> 00:07:01,963
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he doesn't know that the doctor's medicine will help him.

76
00:07:02,063 --> 00:07:08,347
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to find out is first, out of faith, to take it on trust, that he should stop scratching.

77
00:07:08,407 --> 00:07:10,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, he has to take on faith.

78
00:07:11,308 --> 00:07:15,029
[SPEAKER_01]: That the doctor is right and that scratching is the wrong thing to do.

79
00:07:15,169 --> 00:07:19,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's got to keep telling himself, in a way, to really believe the doctor.

80
00:07:19,831 --> 00:07:24,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this scratching is bad, rather than the scratching is good for me.

81
00:07:24,272 --> 00:07:25,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

82
00:07:25,492 --> 00:07:27,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's like you take this... Yeah, exactly.

83
00:07:27,433 --> 00:07:29,154
[SPEAKER_00]: You start rewriting the significance.

84
00:07:29,174 --> 00:07:30,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Rewriting, rewriting, yeah.

85
00:07:31,454 --> 00:07:36,836
[SPEAKER_00]: You take, basically, you take an external view, out of faith, and obviously some consideration.

86
00:07:36,856 --> 00:07:38,036
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to just be faithful.

87
00:07:38,056 --> 00:07:39,697
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to consider it, as we said before,

88
00:07:40,817 --> 00:07:46,420
[SPEAKER_00]: I just blindly take it, but think about it, reason and see which is least likely to be wrong.

89
00:07:46,460 --> 00:07:48,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this doctor... is there a good chance that he might be right?

90
00:07:48,901 --> 00:07:49,781
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I have to lose?

91
00:07:49,881 --> 00:07:50,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way.

92
00:07:51,222 --> 00:07:55,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like, oh, you follow this teacher to the end of the world and, like, for the rest of your life.

93
00:07:55,724 --> 00:07:55,884
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

94
00:07:56,324 --> 00:07:59,246
[SPEAKER_00]: You take on his instruction until you're cured.

95
00:08:00,126 --> 00:08:02,427
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you won't need to have faith in him, because you're cured.

96
00:08:02,447 --> 00:08:03,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know that he was right.

97
00:08:04,008 --> 00:08:04,988
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no room for faith now.

98
00:08:05,008 --> 00:08:08,230
[SPEAKER_00]: He's just confident that what you did on account of that instruction was correct.

99
00:08:09,578 --> 00:08:12,723
[SPEAKER_00]: But even then, so you have to take it externally.

100
00:08:12,743 --> 00:08:15,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your whole condition is affected with the disease, so to speak.

101
00:08:16,570 --> 00:08:18,914
[SPEAKER_00]: So you have no health reference point.

102
00:08:20,176 --> 00:08:22,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Since you can remember, your skin was itching.

103
00:08:24,377 --> 00:08:31,221
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you take it on trust, externally, external point of view, saying, actually, if you stop scratching sufficiently enough, the time will come when you will heal.

104
00:08:32,162 --> 00:08:36,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you value the prospect, the idea of healing, after, you know, thinking about it.

105
00:08:37,445 --> 00:08:38,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you take it on trust.

106
00:08:38,946 --> 00:08:42,289
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whichever view you take on trust, is basically like, like grafting a tree.

107
00:08:43,069 --> 00:08:51,535
[SPEAKER_00]: You take a completely different thing, and then you put it onto this tree, and if you cultivate it rightly enough, it will grow into the tree, and then it will become a new tree.

108
00:08:52,460 --> 00:08:54,021
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like how the grafting is done.

109
00:08:54,682 --> 00:08:58,065
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you have the mix of the previous and the new tree, so to speak.

110
00:08:58,085 --> 00:09:01,068
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to graft the view onto your experience.

111
00:09:01,508 --> 00:09:13,099
[SPEAKER_00]: They will then take over things, because they would... if the view was right, that you took, if the instruction was from the Buddha, for example, and you took it rightly, and you've been restrained, it will have to

112
00:09:14,550 --> 00:09:20,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Take, like, you have to start growing correctly, because the view was right, and that means it corresponds to the universal nature of things.

113
00:09:21,573 --> 00:09:23,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Such as, sensuality was truly unpleasant.

114
00:09:24,195 --> 00:09:26,016
[SPEAKER_00]: It was truly burning embers.

115
00:09:26,436 --> 00:09:28,097
[SPEAKER_00]: It was truly dissatisfactory.

116
00:09:29,017 --> 00:09:38,482
[SPEAKER_00]: And you took that on trust, and then kept applying it, and kept applying it, and keeping the basis of restraint, and then it took root, because those were the exactly matching conditions for the view that you took.

117
00:09:40,129 --> 00:09:40,730
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's same.

118
00:09:40,750 --> 00:09:43,952
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you take the medicine, you follow the doctor's instruction.

119
00:09:44,453 --> 00:09:46,174
[SPEAKER_00]: If it doesn't work, well, you review it.

120
00:09:46,735 --> 00:09:50,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it because I'm not restrained enough, honestly speaking?

121
00:09:50,558 --> 00:09:56,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it because... no, I'm completely restrained, I'm doing everything rightly, but this doesn't seem to be curing me of my itch.

122
00:09:57,885 --> 00:09:59,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, maybe I should look for another doctor.

123
00:10:00,887 --> 00:10:01,628
[SPEAKER_00]: And then do the same.

124
00:10:02,268 --> 00:10:03,670
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you do want results.

125
00:10:04,210 --> 00:10:06,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just take it on faith without any results whatsoever.

126
00:10:07,753 --> 00:10:13,655
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, at the same time, don't forget that you don't really know what the right results are at that stage.

127
00:10:13,915 --> 00:10:23,458
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there has to be some time involved on... during which you're applying these things out of faith and confidence that it will help you.

128
00:10:23,498 --> 00:10:24,458
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't happen overnight.

129
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:29,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it takes...

130
00:10:36,805 --> 00:10:38,445
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just to recap again.

131
00:10:40,606 --> 00:10:46,227
[SPEAKER_01]: These defilements to be abandoned by seeing, by aperception.

132
00:10:46,267 --> 00:10:59,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it says that an ordinary person does not understand what things are fit for attention and what things are unfit for attention.

133
00:11:00,550 --> 00:11:03,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Since that is so, he attends to things that are unfit for attention,

134
00:11:04,058 --> 00:11:06,600
[SPEAKER_01]: And he does not attend to those things fit for attention.

135
00:11:11,303 --> 00:11:14,225
[SPEAKER_01]: And what are the things unfit for attention that he attends to?

136
00:11:14,565 --> 00:11:29,975
[SPEAKER_01]: They are things such that when he attends to them, the unarisen defilement of sensual desire arises, the defilement of being arises, and the defilement of ignorance arises, or increases.

137
00:11:32,456 --> 00:11:33,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, increases.

138
00:11:33,482 --> 00:11:33,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

139
00:11:34,523 --> 00:11:43,810
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he doesn't see what the, you know, what he's attending to is actually causing him these problems.

140
00:11:43,830 --> 00:11:50,475
[SPEAKER_01]: The way he's attending to things is making the farmers worse, increasing them, sustaining them, maintaining them.

141
00:11:51,576 --> 00:11:53,197
[SPEAKER_01]: But the noble disciple

142
00:11:54,457 --> 00:11:59,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he attends to things with a perspective, he can see, ah, this...

143
00:11:59,981 --> 00:12:01,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Suitable and unsuitable.

144
00:12:01,142 --> 00:12:09,229
[SPEAKER_01]: This thing that I attend to now, which is kind of neutral maybe in itself, is actually increasing my sensual desire.

145
00:12:10,850 --> 00:12:12,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you realize it's not neutral.

146
00:12:13,292 --> 00:12:18,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

147
00:12:18,396 --> 00:12:22,980
[SPEAKER_01]: So the noble disciple then has this ability to see his mind

148
00:12:24,167 --> 00:12:36,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about it in the same simile of the skin condition and the medicine from the doctor in the beginning.

149
00:12:37,218 --> 00:12:38,058
[SPEAKER_00]: All you know is itch.

150
00:12:39,039 --> 00:12:40,941
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't really know what's helpful, what's not helpful.

151
00:12:41,741 --> 00:12:49,487
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start restraining, you start putting this cream on, you start not exposing it to sun, cleaning your wound, as the Buddha would say, and so on.

152
00:12:50,648 --> 00:12:51,709
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it starts getting better.

153
00:12:53,793 --> 00:13:02,379
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, you do recognize improvement for yourself, which means you don't need to keep reminding yourself of the faith you have in a doctor, because it's working.

154
00:13:02,999 --> 00:13:04,560
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're still not fully healed.

155
00:13:06,722 --> 00:13:09,244
[SPEAKER_00]: So that, that would be the equivalent of a noble disciple.

156
00:13:10,128 --> 00:13:17,431
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from the point of view of partially healed wound and diminished itch, he knows exactly now, because that's now his criteria.

157
00:13:18,011 --> 00:13:24,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, actually, this is now bringing back that old itch that was gone, that disappeared.

158
00:13:25,074 --> 00:13:28,376
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I do this, then it's making it even less itchy than before.

159
00:13:28,696 --> 00:13:31,777
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's healing more or aggravating it again.

160
00:13:32,618 --> 00:13:37,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And now he can know because he gained some perspective due to the actual healing of his skin condition.

161
00:13:40,969 --> 00:13:46,157
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the noble disciple would know what's fit for attention, what it isn't, because he actually sees it.

162
00:13:46,277 --> 00:13:50,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, if I attend to this, that which hasn't healed yet, it's not gonna heal.

163
00:13:50,864 --> 00:13:53,689
[SPEAKER_00]: If I attend to this, that which hasn't healed yet, is healing more.

164
00:14:03,823 --> 00:14:07,665
[SPEAKER_01]: But also, ordinary people can sort of... can practice that.

165
00:14:07,685 --> 00:14:10,767
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see, well, if I... Well, you should be trying.

166
00:14:10,807 --> 00:14:11,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

167
00:14:11,247 --> 00:14:13,428
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm doing these actions and what is the result of it?

168
00:14:14,009 --> 00:14:17,591
[SPEAKER_01]: More sensual desire, more, you know, distraction, dull-minded laziness.

169
00:14:17,611 --> 00:14:21,613
[SPEAKER_00]: You just... you won't have... you won't have a putujana

170
00:14:22,667 --> 00:14:30,829
[SPEAKER_00]: You can, by all means, and should, by all means, be trying to emulate Sotāpanna, or higher, because that's how you have a chance of understanding it.

171
00:14:31,489 --> 00:14:35,770
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't confuse yourself into, like, believing that you are now, because, look, I'm doing it.

172
00:14:36,510 --> 00:14:44,072
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what, what Putujananda will not have is the immediacy of that criteria that a noble disciple has.

173
00:14:45,093 --> 00:14:46,233
[SPEAKER_00]: He immediately knows

174
00:14:47,375 --> 00:14:53,633
[SPEAKER_00]: He might choose to ignore it, but it's visible, for him it's immediately visible, whether this is suitable or unsuitable.

175
00:14:54,375 --> 00:14:54,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

176
00:14:55,566 --> 00:14:58,808
[SPEAKER_01]: How unsuitable this is, or how suitable this is.

177
00:14:58,828 --> 00:15:00,609
[SPEAKER_00]: But Ujjaina doesn't have that.

178
00:15:00,649 --> 00:15:15,117
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't have the immediacy of that criteria, which means he needs to invest more effort, like wider effort, into constantly recollecting the context of the danger that he took on faith, the context of avoiding this, cultivating that, what doctor told him.

179
00:15:15,878 --> 00:15:22,421
[SPEAKER_00]: He needs to keep more in his mind, because he doesn't have the immediate verification that, oh, look, my wound's getting better, my wound's getting worse.

180
00:15:22,601 --> 00:15:23,542
[SPEAKER_00]: For him, it's all worse.

181
00:15:26,810 --> 00:15:27,391
[SPEAKER_01]: He's suffering.

182
00:15:27,591 --> 00:15:27,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

183
00:15:28,291 --> 00:15:31,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he has no criteria that will come internally from the wound.

184
00:15:32,935 --> 00:15:36,418
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all like what he has adopted as views and faith.

185
00:15:42,790 --> 00:15:52,858
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's something like, as I said, the Noble Disciple doesn't need to remind himself anymore that the doctor knows what he's talking about, because his wound is... that's the significance of his wound now.

186
00:15:52,958 --> 00:15:57,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Healing wound is basically the significance of a doctor was correct.

187
00:15:57,782 --> 00:15:58,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

188
00:15:58,142 --> 00:15:59,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Now he has got a criteria.

189
00:15:59,784 --> 00:16:00,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

190
00:16:00,244 --> 00:16:01,825
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows what healing is.

191
00:16:02,146 --> 00:16:02,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

192
00:16:02,586 --> 00:16:03,106
[SPEAKER_00]: He sees it.

193
00:16:03,547 --> 00:16:04,127
[SPEAKER_01]: He sees it now.

194
00:16:04,147 --> 00:16:09,311
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now he can pick up the sign of unwholesome and wholesome immediately.

195
00:16:11,253 --> 00:16:12,474
[SPEAKER_01]: In anything that he attends to.

196
00:16:14,656 --> 00:16:20,159
[SPEAKER_01]: And by seeing that this is increasing defilements, he doesn't attend to it.

197
00:16:20,559 --> 00:16:22,140
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't go in that direction.

198
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:23,380
[SPEAKER_01]: He chooses the other way.

199
00:16:24,481 --> 00:16:27,863
[SPEAKER_01]: He abandons the defilements because of that seeing.

200
00:16:27,883 --> 00:16:28,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

201
00:16:28,403 --> 00:16:30,224
[SPEAKER_00]: That direct seeing is the direct feedback.

202
00:16:38,459 --> 00:16:51,606
[SPEAKER_01]: And, anyway, here it says that these three āsavas, these three defilements, sensual desire, being and ignorance, and just being, the defilement of being.

203
00:16:52,866 --> 00:16:55,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe you can briefly say what... Well, didn't we do the whole talk on that?

204
00:16:57,369 --> 00:17:01,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Being, non-being, bhāva, vibhāva, yeah, being, existence, yeah.

205
00:17:01,331 --> 00:17:05,693
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any... Well, I don't want to get into that now, because that is... We did the whole talk on that.

206
00:17:09,902 --> 00:17:13,645
[SPEAKER_01]: But anything that increases being, ignorance...

207
00:17:13,866 --> 00:17:18,130
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's basically being is what you have left after you abandon sensuality.

208
00:17:19,030 --> 00:17:20,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are the three types of being.

209
00:17:21,132 --> 00:17:26,177
[SPEAKER_00]: The central being, the material being, and the immaterial being.

210
00:17:28,839 --> 00:17:31,622
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is a central being, being is there.

211
00:17:32,983 --> 00:17:34,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the coarsest type of being.

212
00:17:35,619 --> 00:17:36,820
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not only when you engage.

213
00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:42,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're not free from centrality, you're always... your being is on the plane of centrality, on the plane of the senses.

214
00:17:43,286 --> 00:17:47,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if you have abandoned centrality, then your being is on the plane of the presence of the form.

215
00:17:49,251 --> 00:17:53,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, if you abandon that, then your being is on the level of, you know, the absence of the form and so on.

216
00:17:53,214 --> 00:17:57,478
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, that's just way too abstract for any practical application for somebody who will listen to this talk.

217
00:17:58,058 --> 00:18:01,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, practically speaking, whatever your current situation is,

218
00:18:02,015 --> 00:18:03,636
[SPEAKER_00]: means it exists.

219
00:18:03,676 --> 00:18:04,616
[SPEAKER_00]: It's present, as such.

220
00:18:04,957 --> 00:18:05,497
[SPEAKER_00]: It has appeared.

221
00:18:05,517 --> 00:18:06,477
[SPEAKER_00]: It means being is there.

222
00:18:07,158 --> 00:18:09,999
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not an arahant, means there is a degree of being.

223
00:18:10,659 --> 00:18:13,101
[SPEAKER_00]: And being means misconception, in that sense.

224
00:18:13,301 --> 00:18:14,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Being is unwholesome.

225
00:18:15,542 --> 00:18:15,802
[SPEAKER_00]: I am?

226
00:18:16,322 --> 00:18:16,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

227
00:18:17,203 --> 00:18:19,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever there is a being, there is appropriation.

228
00:18:19,384 --> 00:18:21,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever there is a being, there is a sense of self.

229
00:18:21,185 --> 00:18:22,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever there is a being, there is a sense of I am.

230
00:18:23,746 --> 00:18:24,426
[SPEAKER_00]: And vice versa.

231
00:18:24,787 --> 00:18:27,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever there is a sense of I am, means there is a degree of being there.

232
00:18:28,505 --> 00:18:30,927
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's... and this is saying that the Noble Disciple sees...

233
00:18:31,407 --> 00:18:34,289
[SPEAKER_00]: He would see what would increase that and what wouldn't increase that.

234
00:18:35,530 --> 00:18:36,111
[SPEAKER_00]: I think...

235
00:18:36,131 --> 00:18:42,696
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's... it's a bit coarse to say, oh, sense of self is what the being is, but practically speaking, it is.

236
00:18:42,716 --> 00:18:48,180
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no sense of being or existence that you can perceive apart from your sense of self.

237
00:18:49,141 --> 00:18:54,065
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even in philosophy, people think they're talking about the pure being, independent of an individual.

238
00:18:54,145 --> 00:18:58,108
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a pure abstraction that's within their sense of self and being of it.

239
00:18:59,437 --> 00:19:13,941
[SPEAKER_00]: So, being, bhāva, existence in philosophy, whatever you want to call it, whichever word you want to use, if you are honestly and like strictly looking at it as a phenomena, it's inseparable from the sense of self and your current situation.

240
00:19:14,341 --> 00:19:23,884
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why your current situation will either be on the level of kāma-bhāva, rūpa-bhāva or ārūpa-bhāva, depending on how much effort you made to abandon it.

241
00:19:25,107 --> 00:19:32,274
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, practically speaking, being bhava is that, being of I am, even if you don't explicitly think in terms of yourself.

242
00:19:32,854 --> 00:19:41,462
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact is that your point of view already carries a degree of that self-centeredness and conceiving of I am, that being is there.

243
00:19:42,383 --> 00:19:46,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's subtler than I am such and such, but it's a kind of root of it anyway.

244
00:19:47,666 --> 00:20:01,736
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you want to practically start thinking about being and trying to get a criteria what would increase the being, what would decrease the being, think from that, oh, the self-centeredness, the conceit, the I am, the self, the point of view.

245
00:20:01,856 --> 00:20:02,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

246
00:20:02,537 --> 00:20:06,499
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just reflecting on... Well, exactly.

247
00:20:06,579 --> 00:20:10,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Will this... Like again, is this pleasing to me?

248
00:20:11,003 --> 00:20:12,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this displeasing to me?

249
00:20:12,904 --> 00:20:14,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Why does that matter to me?

250
00:20:16,128 --> 00:20:27,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, in a way, you realize that many, on many instances, you operate, like, throughout your life, just out of these, eh... things that infer from the sense of self.

251
00:20:28,395 --> 00:20:31,777
[SPEAKER_00]: To me and for me, it's like, yeah, but did I ever stop and think, why?

252
00:20:32,598 --> 00:20:35,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is this a problem for me, or why do I want this?

253
00:20:35,580 --> 00:20:41,263
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it just happened that my sense of self wanted this, that was providing me with gratification, and I never questioned it.

254
00:20:43,232 --> 00:20:45,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, stop using your sense of self as a reference point.

255
00:20:46,695 --> 00:20:53,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, to some extent, you're already shifting away from that, like, perpetuating that being with that wrong view.

256
00:20:55,041 --> 00:20:59,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And the ultimate being that is used as a reference point is centrality.

257
00:21:00,085 --> 00:21:01,967
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the first and foremost for most people.

258
00:21:03,388 --> 00:21:04,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Like pleasure, pain.

259
00:21:05,423 --> 00:21:06,264
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the criteria.

260
00:21:06,424 --> 00:21:14,970
[SPEAKER_00]: That's literally the place, the self-centered, kind of the center from which they infer whether something is worth doing or not worth doing.

261
00:21:15,010 --> 00:21:16,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Will this provide me with pleasure?

262
00:21:17,032 --> 00:21:18,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Or will this punish me with pain?

263
00:21:20,194 --> 00:21:25,658
[SPEAKER_00]: But, so, if you stop using that center, you stop relying on Karmabhāva.

264
00:21:25,698 --> 00:21:27,440
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop relying on the central being.

265
00:21:27,980 --> 00:21:30,682
[SPEAKER_00]: And instead, maybe you inform your perspective a bit more.

266
00:21:31,243 --> 00:21:33,585
[SPEAKER_00]: And you say, well, sense restraint will hurt me.

267
00:21:34,430 --> 00:21:44,697
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, but, you know, if it's to believe all these wise men, and even my own reasoning, I think there is a chance that it might heal my my wound of sensual itch, and so on.

268
00:21:45,978 --> 00:21:52,082
[SPEAKER_00]: So, immediately, you're choosing pain, pretty much, which is against the criteria of your kammabhāva and what you are, basically.

269
00:21:53,283 --> 00:22:03,270
[SPEAKER_00]: But, so, see, you're not using that self-centered being of kamma, sensuality, as a reference point anymore, but are actually dissolving it into something more wholesome, something more informed.

270
00:22:06,405 --> 00:22:12,048
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you abandon centrality, you have abandoned kama-bhāva, so your being bhāva has diminished to a great extent.

271
00:22:13,029 --> 00:22:26,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, there are subtler forms of bhāva, but again, you're unable to think about subtler forms of bhāva, unless you're already free from kama-bhāva, because whenever you think about all sorts of types of bhāva, you're thinking it through the framework of bhāva that you are.

272
00:22:26,916 --> 00:22:31,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is, for most people, kāma-bhāva, because most people are not free from sensuality.

273
00:22:31,677 --> 00:22:37,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the lower noble disciples are not free from sensuality, so they're not free from that, from that bhāva yet.

274
00:22:54,142 --> 00:22:56,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it says the ordinary person,

275
00:22:58,755 --> 00:23:05,539
[SPEAKER_01]: This is how he attends unwisely or without perspective.

276
00:23:07,059 --> 00:23:09,621
[SPEAKER_01]: He asks questions internally, so to speak.

277
00:23:09,881 --> 00:23:10,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Was I in the past?

278
00:23:10,821 --> 00:23:11,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Was I not in the past?

279
00:23:14,132 --> 00:23:16,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Having been, what did I become in the past?

280
00:23:16,873 --> 00:23:19,175
[SPEAKER_01]: What shall I be in the future?

281
00:23:19,335 --> 00:23:21,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Having been, what shall I become in the future?

282
00:23:22,617 --> 00:23:25,958
[SPEAKER_01]: Or else, he's inwardly perplexed about the present.

283
00:23:26,118 --> 00:23:26,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, am I?

284
00:23:26,899 --> 00:23:27,359
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I not?

285
00:23:27,599 --> 00:23:28,059
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I?

286
00:23:28,480 --> 00:23:28,960
[SPEAKER_01]: How am I?

287
00:23:29,920 --> 00:23:31,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Where has this being come from?

288
00:23:31,381 --> 00:23:32,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Where will it go?

289
00:23:32,162 --> 00:23:33,202
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a few more.

290
00:23:33,222 --> 00:23:33,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

291
00:23:33,602 --> 00:23:34,483
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I was saying.

292
00:23:34,503 --> 00:23:38,085
[SPEAKER_00]: See, in that instance, just clearly shows how

293
00:23:39,329 --> 00:23:44,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just that you're not free from the gratuitous self-centered sense of I am.

294
00:23:45,591 --> 00:23:46,952
[SPEAKER_00]: I am the center of the world.

295
00:23:47,312 --> 00:23:49,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, was I in the past?

296
00:23:49,112 --> 00:23:54,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is determined by you and whether you were and will you and are you going to be.

297
00:23:54,894 --> 00:23:56,775
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already completely gratuitous.

298
00:23:58,215 --> 00:24:05,198
[SPEAKER_00]: But, so even if you don't necessarily act out of that wrongly assumed center of sense of self,

299
00:24:06,383 --> 00:24:11,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Entertaining these questions still perpetuates the centeredness of that gratuitous view.

300
00:24:12,128 --> 00:24:14,990
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why those are wrong questions, as the Buddha would say.

301
00:24:15,431 --> 00:24:19,674
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because, oh, you know, you can never express what the true self is.

302
00:24:19,715 --> 00:24:19,855
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

303
00:24:20,235 --> 00:24:29,263
[SPEAKER_00]: It's simply because any of those questions still revolves around the assumption that that self is in the center and it's worth answering.

304
00:24:29,323 --> 00:24:31,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Self-centered questioning point of view.

305
00:24:33,363 --> 00:24:35,144
[SPEAKER_00]: So, am I in the past?

306
00:24:35,284 --> 00:24:36,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Was I in the past?

307
00:24:36,124 --> 00:24:37,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I be in the future?

308
00:24:38,166 --> 00:24:46,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Takes for granted that that self is the center of that whole experience and thus it's important for me to know was I in the past and will I be in the future.

309
00:24:47,110 --> 00:24:49,191
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's actually irrelevant.

310
00:24:49,211 --> 00:24:53,974
[SPEAKER_00]: What is relevant is to stop being centered upon that self-center.

311
00:24:54,994 --> 00:24:59,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness is already a way of cultivating and maintaining

312
00:25:00,737 --> 00:25:12,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Or preventing yourself from becoming reabsorbed too deeply into that gratuitously assumed self-centered I am, that just basically always serves its own purpose.

313
00:25:13,749 --> 00:25:17,853
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you live... the noble disciple lives according to...

314
00:25:19,427 --> 00:25:27,911
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as it says, this is how he attends without perspective.

315
00:25:27,951 --> 00:25:32,693
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he, this ordinary person, doesn't have that perspective of the nature of things.

316
00:25:33,634 --> 00:25:35,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And thus, he thinks he's the center, he's the

317
00:25:36,202 --> 00:25:37,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's, that's implicit.

318
00:25:37,723 --> 00:25:38,883
[SPEAKER_00]: That's part of the significance.

319
00:25:38,903 --> 00:25:40,024
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't even need to think it.

320
00:25:40,084 --> 00:25:44,045
[SPEAKER_00]: He needs to actively think the opposite, to eventually undo that significance.

321
00:25:44,065 --> 00:25:44,826
[SPEAKER_01]: But one could do...

322
00:25:44,906 --> 00:25:47,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha says this, yet I'm doing this.

323
00:25:47,667 --> 00:25:47,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

324
00:25:48,087 --> 00:25:51,328
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let me have faith and do what he says, not what...

325
00:25:51,408 --> 00:25:52,909
[SPEAKER_01]: I put myself second.

326
00:25:53,009 --> 00:26:01,252
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why faith, as blind as it is, always, in its nature, can be a factor that can, that can help somebody with the right view.

327
00:26:01,513 --> 00:26:02,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Not in itself,

328
00:26:02,633 --> 00:26:12,744
[SPEAKER_00]: But, exactly, if somebody out of faith, firm faith, decides to use Buddha's reference point from the suttas and not his reference point.

329
00:26:12,764 --> 00:26:15,467
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I want to do this, the Buddha says you shouldn't do it, and I don't do it.

330
00:26:16,027 --> 00:26:16,788
[SPEAKER_00]: And you stick to it.

331
00:26:16,808 --> 00:26:20,972
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to take longer than if you make more active effort in terms of vipassana.

332
00:26:21,673 --> 00:26:23,474
[SPEAKER_00]: But nevertheless, you are doing the work.

333
00:26:24,134 --> 00:26:26,375
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why there is the Sadhanussari type.

334
00:26:26,835 --> 00:26:40,842
[SPEAKER_00]: The one, through faith, adopts certain behavior and sticks to it, and it goes against his entire grain of his being, but he sticks to it, and that being will evaporate sufficiently, and he becomes Sotāpanna, like Sadhanussari, that leads to the right view.

335
00:26:43,703 --> 00:26:48,345
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, whether you... and for most people, it would be a combination of a bit of everything.

336
00:26:48,964 --> 00:26:54,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, as we've said in the past, or I've said in the past as well, in the suttas it's clear,

337
00:27:05,805 --> 00:27:08,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Like sexual activity needs to be abandoned.

338
00:27:08,907 --> 00:27:09,528
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unwholesome.

339
00:27:09,728 --> 00:27:10,369
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha says it.

340
00:27:10,529 --> 00:27:12,410
[SPEAKER_01]: For the practice of dhamma, you cannot have it.

341
00:27:12,790 --> 00:27:20,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, for many people who go, yeah, but well, you know, hey, you know, you know, well, it's a case... Means they don't have enough faith.

342
00:27:20,396 --> 00:27:20,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

343
00:27:20,837 --> 00:27:24,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if they did, they wouldn't even need to reason.

344
00:27:24,700 --> 00:27:26,901
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, some people need to reason.

345
00:27:27,402 --> 00:27:30,024
[SPEAKER_00]: I personally need to reason or needed to reason things.

346
00:27:30,785 --> 00:27:33,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Faith wasn't really working to that extent.

347
00:27:34,162 --> 00:27:42,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Whichever way it is, you do need to start emulating that behavior, either on account of faith or on account of your reasoning, but you need to start doing it.

348
00:27:43,145 --> 00:27:50,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like, oh, I have faith, the Buddha is the greatest, but does your behavior emulate what the great Buddha said or not?

349
00:27:51,192 --> 00:27:52,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like, yes, I've reasoned everything.

350
00:27:52,693 --> 00:27:54,194
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha is perfectly right, again.

351
00:27:54,954 --> 00:27:57,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Does your behavior emulate now that rightness that you reasoned?

352
00:27:58,257 --> 00:27:58,397
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

353
00:27:58,637 --> 00:28:00,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you will not bear the result.

354
00:28:00,618 --> 00:28:04,361
[SPEAKER_00]: It's again like the simile the Buddha gave, like a hen that sits on her eggs.

355
00:28:05,101 --> 00:28:11,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if she doesn't wish for the chicks to pierce, she is making the effort, doing the work and sitting on her eggs.

356
00:28:11,305 --> 00:28:14,247
[SPEAKER_00]: They will come out safely and be born into this world.

357
00:28:16,274 --> 00:28:22,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Alternatively, you have the hand that doesn't sit on her eggs, doesn't do the work, but she wishes that her chicks come out in this world.

358
00:28:22,801 --> 00:28:23,241
[SPEAKER_00]: They won't.

359
00:28:23,902 --> 00:28:25,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Wishing is irrelevant.

360
00:28:25,663 --> 00:28:28,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you wish or not, it's basically whether you do it or not.

361
00:28:28,646 --> 00:28:29,687
[SPEAKER_00]: That's going to bear the results.

362
00:28:30,208 --> 00:28:34,953
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said that the dhamma and the practice and the results of dhamma are not to be obtained by the wishing alone.

363
00:28:36,846 --> 00:28:42,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can wish, and because of the wish, you start making the effort and sit on the eggs until they hatch.

364
00:28:43,252 --> 00:28:49,497
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you just wish and glorify your wish, as most people's faith practice these days is, you're not doing the work.

365
00:28:49,877 --> 00:28:56,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's exactly... Oh, I'm so devoted, I'm so proud, I'm so happy, I'm so inspired.

366
00:28:57,364 --> 00:28:58,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you keep the five precepts?

367
00:28:58,465 --> 00:28:59,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you keep the eight precepts?

368
00:28:59,546 --> 00:29:00,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you celibate?

369
00:29:00,187 --> 00:29:01,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you eat once a day?

370
00:29:01,648 --> 00:29:03,129
[SPEAKER_00]: How much time do you spend in solitude?

371
00:29:03,209 --> 00:29:04,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you read the suttas?

372
00:29:04,430 --> 00:29:05,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no, no, no, no, no.

373
00:29:05,951 --> 00:29:07,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I have such a great faith.

374
00:29:07,272 --> 00:29:08,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that faith is meaningless.

375
00:29:08,272 --> 00:29:10,093
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, I have a faith in this doctor.

376
00:29:10,393 --> 00:29:18,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but I'm not neither abstaining from from scratching, neither covering it from the sun, neither cleaning it from the dust, neither applying the medicine.

377
00:29:19,097 --> 00:29:22,119
[SPEAKER_00]: I just have a shrine that venerates this great doctor.

378
00:29:23,019 --> 00:29:24,620
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, okay.

379
00:29:25,440 --> 00:29:28,342
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you cannot possibly expect to heal.

380
00:29:30,176 --> 00:29:43,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Just remind me, also, the person with faith, real faith, proper faith, he takes on that idea, he has that idea, the Buddha is the teacher, I am the disciple, is one of the suttas.

381
00:29:43,265 --> 00:29:44,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

382
00:29:44,626 --> 00:29:45,447
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows what he's taught.

383
00:29:45,587 --> 00:29:46,047
[SPEAKER_01]: I do not know.

384
00:29:46,087 --> 00:29:46,587
[SPEAKER_01]: I do not know.

385
00:29:47,548 --> 00:29:51,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Even though I believe this way, but he's saying to go this way.

386
00:29:51,931 --> 00:29:53,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the Buddha is the teacher.

387
00:29:53,172 --> 00:29:54,813
[SPEAKER_00]: If things are at odds, I know who's right.

388
00:29:55,053 --> 00:29:55,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

389
00:29:56,734 --> 00:29:57,435
[SPEAKER_01]: If things are... yeah.

390
00:30:00,518 --> 00:30:15,409
[SPEAKER_01]: So again, just to continue on that ordinary person, when he attends without perspective, one of six views arise in him.

391
00:30:18,532 --> 00:30:19,953
[SPEAKER_01]: The self exists for me.

392
00:30:22,240 --> 00:30:24,401
[SPEAKER_01]: No self exists for me.

393
00:30:24,581 --> 00:30:26,902
[SPEAKER_01]: I perceive self with self.

394
00:30:27,562 --> 00:30:29,522
[SPEAKER_01]: I perceive not-self with self.

395
00:30:30,563 --> 00:30:33,204
[SPEAKER_01]: I perceive self with not-self.

396
00:30:33,524 --> 00:30:38,846
[SPEAKER_01]: It is this self of mine that speaks and feels and experiences here and now the results of good and bad actions.

397
00:30:38,886 --> 00:30:45,548
[SPEAKER_01]: But this self of mine is permanent, everlasting, eternal, not subject to change, and it will endure as long as eternity.

398
00:30:46,280 --> 00:30:51,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, all of that is on the level of significance of your experience, of your self-centered experience.

399
00:30:51,962 --> 00:30:54,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Those are not explicitly thought that people will have in their mind.

400
00:30:54,403 --> 00:30:55,163
[SPEAKER_00]: I will live forever.

401
00:30:55,203 --> 00:30:55,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody thinks that.

402
00:30:56,324 --> 00:30:59,665
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, everybody is a victim of their view, unless you're a sotāpanna.

403
00:31:00,705 --> 00:31:02,886
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what you need to understand when you read these suttas.

404
00:31:02,946 --> 00:31:04,367
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, it's gonna make sense.

405
00:31:04,547 --> 00:31:05,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, okay.

406
00:31:05,027 --> 00:31:07,208
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's like, yeah, but I don't partake on this level.

407
00:31:08,097 --> 00:31:13,020
[SPEAKER_00]: This is for somebody who already understood, and he's describing how, when you boil down things, how they're seen.

408
00:31:13,680 --> 00:31:15,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Practically speaking, you don't see that.

409
00:31:15,341 --> 00:31:19,284
[SPEAKER_00]: You are a victim already, of your own delusion, of your own ignorance.

410
00:31:20,324 --> 00:31:27,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, practically speaking, if you want to understand these six, basically, products, you should see that, what do they all have together, in common?

411
00:31:28,570 --> 00:31:33,973
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that self, it's basically, not a problem, but the result of a problem.

412
00:31:35,086 --> 00:31:36,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Your sense of self is a symptom.

413
00:31:37,287 --> 00:31:40,928
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a symptom of your... your itch is a symptom of your skin condition.

414
00:31:41,768 --> 00:31:43,269
[SPEAKER_00]: In itself, it's not the issue.

415
00:31:45,189 --> 00:31:52,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can itch and scratch and so on, but unless you uproot the underlying cause, you're still under the same condition.

416
00:31:53,862 --> 00:32:02,291
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sense of self, I am, I am not, will I be, all of that is already the result of that self-centeredness that we mentioned before.

417
00:32:02,691 --> 00:32:04,313
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the problem is.

418
00:32:04,773 --> 00:32:15,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Only seeing, or basically, mostly seeing things through your own point of view that's fueled by greed, aversion, delusion, seeking the pleasure, avoiding pain, and distracting yourself from neutral.

419
00:32:16,460 --> 00:32:24,525
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why sense-restraint, like virtue and sense-restraint, is already kind of forcing you to go against the grain of your sense of self.

420
00:32:25,165 --> 00:32:36,532
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's actually psychologically frightening for people to, like, even if they're not celibate, just the idea of celibacy or the idea of not scratching their itch freaks them out.

421
00:32:36,752 --> 00:32:41,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's like too much pain to bear, because their sense of self is so dependent upon those outlets.

422
00:32:42,580 --> 00:32:48,122
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you actually start practicing that, you are already undermining that gratuitous self-centeredness.

423
00:32:48,842 --> 00:32:54,844
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your sense of self is diminishing, even if you don't necessarily, you know, measure it or you can quantify it or something.

424
00:32:56,004 --> 00:32:59,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about finding the answer to what myself and this myself.

425
00:32:59,746 --> 00:33:05,368
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about uprooting things sufficiently enough until those six types of perceiving it are dissolved.

426
00:33:05,468 --> 00:33:06,088
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you know it.

427
00:33:06,928 --> 00:33:09,289
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, what's left is with not-self, you see not-self.

428
00:33:13,073 --> 00:33:13,304
That's it.

429
00:33:13,324 --> 00:33:13,597
That's it.

